Supporting material showcased in isolation — figures, tables, diagrams, and framed boxes.

<section #ag-caption | `<caption>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<caption>`.

</section>

<section #ag-diagram | `<diagram>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<diagram>`.

<code-block #code:ag-diagram-1>
<diagram mermaid>
graph LR
  A[Start] --> B{Decision}
  B -->|yes| C[OK]
  B -->|no| D[Stop]
</diagram>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:ag-diagram-1>
<diagram mermaid>
graph LR
  A[Start] --> B{Decision}
  B -->|yes| C[OK]
  B -->|no| D[Stop]
</diagram>
</minipage>

*The `mermaid` format word selects the Mermaid engine. The handler
emits the same `&lt;pre class="mermaid" data-enscribe-dsl="mermaid"&gt;`
contract as the legacy `&lt;mermaid&gt;` shorthand expands to.*

</section>

<section #ag-fig | `<fig>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<fig>`.

<code #code:ag-fig-1>
<fig src=elephant.jpg | An adult African elephant.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:ag-fig-1>
<fig src=elephant.jpg | An adult African elephant.>
</minipage>

*The simplest case. The src kwarg generates the &lt;img&gt;; the pipe
content generates the figcaption. The alt text defaults to the
figcaption text when not specified explicitly. The eHTML
element is HTML-native &lt;figure&gt; (not the custom-element &lt;fig&gt;)
because the HTML rendering surface is the HTML5 native element
while the enscribe canonical name follows JATS's shorter &lt;fig&gt;.*

</section>

<section #ag-frame | `<frame>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<frame>`.

<code #code:ag-frame-1>
<frame | A short callout.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:ag-frame-1>
<frame | A short callout.>
</minipage>

*The simplest case. The handler emits a &lt;figure&gt; wrapper (the vocab
html_output.element `frame` is only the lookup key for handler-strategy
entries — the handler controls the actual element). +border is default on
for &lt;frame&gt;, so the class appears automatically.*

</section>

<section #ag-minipage | `<minipage>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<minipage>`.

<code #code:ag-minipage-1>
<minipage | Two panels side by side.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:ag-minipage-1>
<minipage | Two panels side by side.>
</minipage>

*The simplest case. The handler emits a &lt;figure&gt; wrapper (the vocab
html_output.element `minipage` is only the lookup key for handler-strategy
entries — the handler controls the actual element). +border is default on
for &lt;minipage&gt;, so the class appears automatically. The body renders as
sealed eHTML.*

</section>

<section #ag-svg | `<svg>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<svg>`.

<code-block #code:ag-svg-1>
<svg -numbered viewBox="0 0 100 100" width=200 height=200 |
  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" fill="blue" />
>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:ag-svg-1>
<svg -numbered viewBox="0 0 100 100" width=200 height=200 |
  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" fill="blue" />
>
</minipage>

*A lone inline SVG. `&lt;svg&gt;` is numbered by default (it shares the
figure counter), so `-numbered` is what opts out of framing for a
purely inline graphic. The source is opaque pipe content; the
attributes pass through to the rendered &lt;svg&gt; element.*

</section>

<section #ag-table | `<table>`>

Compiles to eHTML `<table>`.

<code-block #code:ag-table-1>
| Name | Price |
|------|-------|
| foo  | 1     |
| bar  | 2     |
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:ag-table-1>
| Name | Price |
|------|-------|
| foo  | 1     |
| bar  | 2     |
</minipage>

*Plain markdown table syntax (via remark-gfm). The most common
authoring path for simple tables. No explicit enscribe tags needed.*

</section>
